speechtext:
Any man who suggests that men of the type who lead the legion, Saskatchewan command, would let a little pique influence them in deciding what they thought was best for their comrades is to do them far less than justice. I am astonished at my hon. friend throwing out that suggestion, knowing the members of the legion in Saskatchewan as he does. I am sure he knows in his own heart that men of the type they are would not let a little pique influence them and would not permit themselves to be manipulated behind the scenes.
There is another matter which I find quite significant. It has been suggested that the legion as a whole is in favour of this programme. I may be wrong in what I am going to say, and I can be corrected if I am;

but I have gone very carefully through the Legionary of June, 1946, and read the resolutions passed at the last national convention of the legion, on May 19 to 23, and after all, if there is one authentic way in which the legion speaks it must be through its national organization meeting in biennial convention. I have examined the resolutions from start to finish to see if the national convention followed the lead of the Saskatchewan command or if they followed the lead of my hon. friend the member for Battle River. I was interested to see that, as far as I could find, this matter into which some hon. members have gone with-I do not know what I should call it; not ecstasy, but the very opposite-
